  "id": "asiko_lagos",
  "name": "Aṣíkò (Lagos frame-drum dance music)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Dance music associated with Christian Yoruba communities in early twentieth-century Lagos, played on frame drums with group singing, which Christopher Waterman identifies in Jùjú: A Social History and Ethnography of an African Popular Music (1990) as one of the precursors of jùjú. It was recorded on 78 rpm discs from the 1930s to the 1950s. The frame-drums card stands in for the aṣíkò drums.",
  "recipe": "classic West African mid-1930s aṣíkò, groove Percussion, west African branch. single leader group response, tonal language syllable tone locked melody. credited frame drums. decca west africa subsidiary 1947-1975 mid tier lagos decca west african studio. large-diaphragm condenser, tube pre, German tube console. 78rpm shellac.",
